  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Over seventeen hundred people died overnight without a single injury. In 1986, rescuers entered Cameroon\u2019s Lake Nyos valley expecting a violent attack.",
        "visual_keywords": "foggy lake mountains"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Instead, they found intact homes and livestock, but entire towns frozen where people sat.",
        "visual_keywords": "empty village streets"
      },
      {
        "narration": "No fire, no blood, no signs of struggle. How could thousands of lives vanish in total silence?",
        "visual_keywords": "abandoned house interior"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Scientists discovered a rare limnic eruption. Heavy carbon dioxide had trapped beneath the volcanic lake for decades, building massive pressure.",
        "visual_keywords": "volcanic lake aerial"
      },
      {
        "narration": "What triggered the gas release is unknown, though many geologists suspect a landslide, while others suggest a small volcanic eruption, cool rainwater, or natural internal destabilization without an external trigger.",
        "visual_keywords": "mist rolling down hill"
      }
    ],
